::heading level=1 id=tutorial-title
Writing a dx document
::end

::paragraph id=tutorial-intro
Every block starts with `::kind`, carries an `id`, and ends with `::end`. That is the whole grammar. This page walks the blocks you will actually reach for — prose, lists, a table, a drawing, and values you can write into a sentence.
::end

::numbered-list id=tutorial-steps
- Open with a `heading` and a `paragraph`, so the page says what it is.
- Use `bulleted-list`, `numbered-list`, or `checklist` for the working parts.
- Put a table in an `html` block — it is rendered, not quoted.
- Draw with an `svg` block, in `currentColor`, so it reads on paper and in the dark.
- Keep values in a `script` block and write them into prose with `{{name}}`.
- Mark a `code` block `run` when the document should compute its own results.
::end

::heading level=2 id=tutorial-commands-heading
What the editor gives you
::end

::html id=tutorial-shortcuts
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>DX: Show Rendered Document</td><td>Read the .dx as a page.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>DX: Edit Source</td><td>Go back to the blocks behind it.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>DX: Run Code Blocks</td><td>Execute every block marked <code>run</code> and keep the output.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>DX: Export as Image</td><td>Write the page out as a PNG.</td></tr>
		<tr><td>DX: Copy as Markdown</td><td>Take the document somewhere that only speaks Markdown.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
::end

::heading level=2 id=tutorial-values-heading
Values written into a sentence
::end

::script id=tutorial-vars type=application/json
{"phase":"authoring","target":"clean examples"}
::end

::paragraph id=tutorial-vars-preview
A `script` block holds the values; the prose names them. Current phase: {{phase}}. Current target: {{target}}. Change the JSON and every sentence that mentions it follows.
::end

::heading level=2 id=tutorial-drawing-heading
A drawing that belongs to the page
::end

::paragraph id=tutorial-drawing-lead
An `svg` block painted in `currentColor` takes the page's own ink, so one drawing is correct in both palettes and nothing has to detect a theme.
::end

